---
import '../styles/about.css';
---

<section id="about" class="section about" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="margin-layout about-layout">
      <h2 id="about-heading" class="section-heading margin-main">About</h2>

      <div class="about-row">
        <p class="margin-main about-para">
          I'm a full stack developer who thinks carefully about what software is actually <em>for</em>.
          I work across the frontend and the backend<sup class="note-mark" aria-hidden="true">1</sup><button
            type="button"
            class="note-toggle"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="sidenote-1"
          ><span class="sr-only">Note </span>1</button>, but the thread running through all of it is how
          technology shapes the people who use it.
        </p>
        <aside id="sidenote-1" class="margin-note sidenote">
          <span class="sidenote-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
          Mostly Vue and Astro up front, Node and Python behind them, running on AWS.
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="about-row">
        <p class="margin-main about-para">
          That interest led me to publish writing on AI ethics in <em>Becoming Human</em><sup
            class="note-mark"
            aria-hidden="true">2</sup><button
            type="button"
            class="note-toggle"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="sidenote-2"
          ><span class="sr-only">Note </span>2</button> and to pursue philosophy alongside my engineering
          work. I think the best developers aren't only technically sharp. They ask harder questions about
          systems, incentives, and consequences.
        </p>
        <aside id="sidenote-2" class="margin-note sidenote">
          <span class="sidenote-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
          <a
            href="https://becominghuman.ai/the-ethical-computer-professional-625e6c65923b"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >“The Ethical Computer Professional”<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>, <cite>Becoming Human</cite>, 2021.
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="about-row">
        <p class="margin-main about-para">
          Day to day I use AI tools throughout my workflow, from code generation to research to
          documentation. I keep a critical eye on where automation helps and where human judgement is
          irreplaceable<sup class="note-mark" aria-hidden="true">3</sup><button
            type="button"
            class="note-toggle"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="sidenote-3"
          ><span class="sr-only">Note </span>3</button>. That balance is what I bring to every project.
        </p>
        <aside id="sidenote-3" class="margin-note sidenote">
          <span class="sidenote-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
          Where I draw that line: see <a href="#ai-adoption">AI in my workflow</a>.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Mobile sidenotes: the superscript marker becomes a toggle that opens its note
  // inline below the paragraph. On wide screens CSS hides the toggles and the notes
  // sit in the margin. Without scripting, CSS leaves every note visible inline.
  const about = document.getElementById('about');
  if (about) {
    about.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.note-toggle').forEach((button) => {
      const note = document.getElementById(button.getAttribute('aria-controls') ?? '');
      if (!note) return;
      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const open = button.getAttribute('aria-expanded') !== 'true';
        button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(open));
        note.classList.toggle('is-open', open);
      });
    });
  }
</script>
